From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SplitPlay <splitplay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] splitplay: live stream stats in heartbeats,
 client_connected/disconnected

The heartbeat's encoder metrics were always 0. Fill them from the stream
internals and describe every running stream, so that SplitPlay can show
which Moonlight device is on a desk and how the stream is doing
(docs/contracts/ipc.md).

New module src/splitplay/live_stats.{h,cpp}. The streaming threads only
bump counters; rates, percentiles and JSON are computed by the heartbeat
thread once per interval:

- fps: video frames handed to the network by videoBroadcastThread (one
  per packet_t, repeated frames included).
- bitrate_kbps: video bytes actually put on the wire for those frames:
  every FEC block's shards x (prefix + block size), i.e. data and parity
  shards with RTP/NV headers and encryption prefixes.
- encode_ms_p50/p99: host capture-to-send latency. The DDA/WGC capture
  timestamp (QPC of the present) to the moment videoBroadcastThread picks
  the encoded frame up, the same value Sunshine already puts into
  frame_processing_latency for Moonlight's "host processing latency".
  Repeated frames carry no capture timestamp and are left out.
- loss_pct: frames the client reports lost / frames sent in the interval.
  Current Moonlight never sends IDX_LOSS_STATS to Sunshine (it sends
  periodic pings and per-frame FEC status instead), so the source is its
  reference frame invalidation requests (IDX_INVALIDATE_REF_FRAMES,
  frames first..last, overlaps counted once); a legacy IDX_LOSS_STATS
  count is added when one arrives. Loss that is recovered by FEC is not
  loss here, and IDR-only recovery (no RFI) carries no frame range and is
  not counted.
- rtt_ms (new): ENetPeer::roundTripTime of the stream's control peer,
  read by controlBroadcastThread, the thread that services the ENet host.

With several streams on one desk: fps and rtt_ms are the highest of the
streams, bitrate_kbps their sum, loss and encode percentiles pooled.
rtt_ms is null while no stream has a measured round trip. clients is
the number of streams listed in "sessions".

The heartbeat gets a "sessions" array with one entry per running stream:
id (the RTSP launch session id), client and client_uuid (the paired
client owning the launch's TLS client certificate, looked up in
make_launch_session; null when unknown), address,
width/height/fps_requested, codec (h264, hevc, av1),
bitrate_kbps_requested (Moonlight's configured bitrate, before the host's
FEC and audio adjustments), hdr (10-bit stream requested), started_at,
plus the per-stream fps, bitrate_kbps, encode_ms_p50/p99, loss_pct and
rtt_ms (null until the control peer connects).

A stream is reported once stream::session::start() has succeeded and
its client has connected the ENet control peer (the first traffic after
RTSP), so a launch whose client never gets through (encoder failure,
UDP blocked) is never shown. Events: client_connected with the session
fields at that point, client_disconnected when stream::session::join()
has stopped the stream's threads. An ended stream is marked finished
even if it was never reported, so a stop racing start() cannot leave a
ghost.

started(), control_connected() and ended() only queue the events under
a short, I/O-free lock (they run on RTSP and control threads, inside
_session_slots). The heartbeat thread takes the queue and the stream
list in one step, then writes the events followed by the heartbeat, so
a heartbeat never contradicts an event sent before it and a connect is
reported within one heartbeat interval. The heartbeat thread no longer
calls rtsp_stream::session_count() (which joins stopped streams); the
RTSP thread reaps them within 500 ms.

Cost on the hot path: per sent frame two relaxed atomic adds and, for
frames with a capture timestamp, one uncontended lock to store a 16-bit
sample (at most 1024 per interval). The control thread stores one atomic
per loop and per loss report; loss ranges outside 0..UINT32_MAX are
ignored.

